/// Block entities owned by plugins: a block entity type, and data a plugin keeps at a position.
///
/// A block entity of a plugin type holds two compound tags the server never interprets:
///
/// * the **save data**, written to the chunk with the entity and given back when the chunk loads.
///   It never reaches clients.
/// * the optional **client data** (the "update tag" of a vanilla block entity), sent to every
///   player that watches the chunk, in the chunk itself and whenever the entity changes
///   (`set-block-entity-data`, `sync-block-entity`). Block entities without client data are not
///   sent, a client creates the block entity of a block that has one on its own.
///
/// # Types
///
/// Block entity types are a registry (`minecraft:block_entity_type`) that clients can synchronise.
/// The type registered as the n-th gets the id `get-vanilla-block-entity-type-count() + n` (0 for
/// the first type registered by any plugin), which is the id used in the chunk packet and in
/// `ClientboundBlockEntityData`, and the server never reuses or changes an id while it runs.
/// Plugins that register types must load in a stable order if clients depend on the ids.
/// Registration is only possible until the server starts accepting connections (plugin load and
/// `server-load-event`), and needs the `registry.block-entities` permission.
///
/// # Lifecycle
///
/// The entity is stored per chunk and saved in the chunk's `block_entities` list with its full key
/// as `id`, its position, and the data. When a chunk loads whose entity has a type that is no longer
/// registered (the plugin is gone) the entity is skipped with a warning and its data stays in the
/// chunk untouched. The server removes the entity when its block is broken or replaced by another
/// block (not when only the block state changes) and fires `block-entity-unload-event` with
/// `removed` true. `block-entity-load-event` and `block-entity-unload-event` tell the plugin when
/// chunks bring entities in and out, so it can rebuild what it keeps in memory.
///
/// Placing a block does not create its entity: the plugin calls `set-block-entity-data` once the
/// block is in the world.
///
/// # Ticking
///
/// The server does not tick plugin block entities unless the plugin opts a type in with
/// `set-block-entity-ticking`. Then, every server tick and for every world, the server collects the
/// entities of that type that are loaded, in a chunk that ticks (near players, like the vanilla
/// block entities), and due, and fires one `block-entity-tick-event` per world and type with all
/// their positions, so a thousand machines cost one call into the plugin. An entity is due every
/// `interval` ticks, offset by its position so that the entities of a type do not all fall due in
/// the same tick. Nothing is fired for a type without due entities, and nothing is collected while
/// no type has an interval or nobody listens to the event.
interface plugin-block-entity {
    use common.{block-pos, nbt-tree};
    use %world.{%world};

    /// A block entity type and its network id.
    record block-entity-type-entry {
        /// The full resource location, `namespace:path`.
        key: string,
        id: u32,
        /// The keys of the blocks the type belongs to.
        valid-blocks: list<string>,
    }

    /// The data of a block entity of a plugin type.
    record plugin-block-entity-data {
        /// The key of the type.
        type-key: string,
        /// Saved with the chunk, never sent to clients. A compound tag.
        save-data: nbt-tree,
        /// Sent to the players watching the chunk. A compound tag.
        client-data: option<nbt-tree>,
    }

    /// Registers a block entity type and returns its id. `key` is a full resource location,
    /// `namespace:path`, lowercase, `a-z 0-9 _ - .` (and `/` in the path); the `minecraft`
    /// namespace is reserved. `valid-block-keys` names the blocks the type is for; a vanilla block
    /// may omit `minecraft:`, a custom block has to name its namespace (an unqualified
    /// `funnel` would never match `create:funnel`). Fails for an unqualified custom block instead
    /// of accepting a key that can never match. The server uses the list to warn when data is set
    /// at a block that is not listed. Registering the same definition again returns the same id (a
    /// reloaded plugin), a different definition for a known key fails. Fails for an invalid key, or
    /// when registration is closed.
    register-block-entity-type: func(key: string, valid-block-keys: list<string>) -> result<u32, string>;

    /// Returns the id of a plugin block entity type by key.
    get-block-entity-type-id: func(key: string) -> option<u32>;

    /// Returns the number of vanilla block entity types. This is also the id of the first plugin
    /// type.
    get-vanilla-block-entity-type-count: func() -> u32;

    /// Returns all plugin block entity types in id order.
    get-registered-block-entity-types: func() -> list<block-entity-type-entry>;

    /// Sets how often the entities of a type are ticked, in ticks: 1 is every tick, 20 about once a
    /// second. 0 stops ticking the type, which is also the default. The entities of the type are
    /// reported to `block-entity-tick-event`, see "Ticking". Can be called at any time, a later
    /// call replaces the interval. Needs the `registry.block-entities` permission. Fails if the type
    /// is not registered.
    set-block-entity-ticking: func(type-key: string, interval: u32) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Returns the data of the plugin block entity at a position, none if there is none (also when
    /// the chunk is not loaded).
    get-block-entity-data: func(target-world: borrow<%world>, pos: block-pos) -> option<plugin-block-entity-data>;

    /// Creates the block entity at a position, or replaces the data of the one that is there (its
    /// type has to be the same), and sends the client data to the players watching the chunk. Fails
    /// if the type is not registered, the chunk is not loaded, a tree is not a compound tag, or the
    /// position holds a block entity of another type.
    set-block-entity-data: func(target-world: borrow<%world>, pos: block-pos, type-key: string, save-data: nbt-tree, client-data: option<nbt-tree>) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Sends the client data of the plugin block entity at a position to the players watching the
    /// chunk again, and marks the chunk as changed. Fails if there is no such entity.
    sync-block-entity: func(target-world: borrow<%world>, pos: block-pos) -> result<_, string>;

    /// Removes the plugin block entity at a position, with its saved data. Returns whether there
    /// was one. Fires `block-entity-unload-event` with `removed` true.
    ///
    /// Async, like every other import that runs server work: the unload event goes back into the
    /// plugin, and a callback can only be serviced while the calling guest call is suspended.
    remove-block-entity: async func(target-world: borrow<%world>, pos: block-pos) -> bool;
}
